{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q009_heme_bundle_fold_not_homology","answer":"No. SCOPe does not support treating these three groups as one homologous superfamily. In SCOPe release 2.08 (stable; the service reports 2.08 as current public release, and I did not query the 2.08-2026-08-16 periodic update), all three sit under one fold, f.21 'Heme-binding four-helical bundle', but in three separate superfamilies: f.21.1 'Transmembrane di-heme cytochromes' (sunid 81342), f.21.2 'Fumarate reductase respiratory complex transmembrane subunits' (sunid 81343), and f.21.3 'Respiratory nitrate reductase 1 gamma chain' (sunid 103501). The fumarate-reductase transmembrane subunits in the prompt correspond to f.21.2, which holds families f.21.2.1 'Fumarate reductase respiratory complex cytochrome b subunit, FrdC' (56910) and f.21.2.2 'Succinate dehydrogenase/Fumarate reductase transmembrane subunits (SdhC/FrdC and SdhD/FrdD)' (81373). The nitrate reductase gamma chain corresponds to f.21.3, with family f.21.3.1 (sunids 103502/103503).

Interpretation: SCOPe's homology assertion applies within each superfamily (homology_implied_by_level is true for f.21.1, f.21.2 and f.21.3 separately). The fold node f.21 has homology_implied_by_level false and an info warning that levels above superfamily do not imply homology. So the shared heme-binding four-helical transmembrane architecture supports only the fold-level structural classification, not common ancestry across the three superfamilies. The correct statement is that SCOPe makes no positive superfamily-level homology assertion between them. It does not establish that they are unrelated, analogous, or convergent, and I did not gather independent evidence to decide either way.

Caveats: (1) The f.21.3 annotation comment calls the gamma subunit 'a possible link between the two other superfamilies' and says it is 'structurally closer to the Fumarate reductase subunit FrdC'. This is a curator comment, not a SCOPe homology placement, and it is not a merger of the superfamilies. It also says it corresponds to a functionally related formate dehydrogenase N gamma subunit; I did not verify that functional claim against any retrieved source. The f.21.3 Pfam PF02665 xref is an automatic mapping. (2) The f.21.1 annotation says three of four heme ligands are conserved between 'the two families' and that both hemes bind similarly but not identically; this is a structural comment that does not by itself create a superfamily relationship. (3) I did not inspect any FATCAT or BLAST chain hits, residue-level alignments, or topology comparisons, so no regional or structural-alignment evidence was used. Establishing a non-official remote-homology hypothesis would require those comparisons with release-matched lineages, which were not retrieved here. (4) Exact-name searches for 'nitrate reductase gamma chain', 'di-heme cytochrome' and 'heme-binding four-helical' returned empty pages; the nodes were found via the 'fumarate' and 'nitrate' token searches. Those empty results are search-window limits, not evidence of absence.","evidence_used":["scop-homology/SKILL.md","mcp__scope__get_scop_service_info","mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","/api/v1/releases/current","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=fumarate&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=nitrate&mode=auto&limit=20","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344/children?limit=50","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81343/children?limit=50","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81344/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81342 with /parents, /annotations, /homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/103501 with /parents, /annotations, /homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/81373 with /parents, /annotations, /homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/56910 with /parents, /annotations, /homology"]}]}
